I really enjoyed this. As Taffeta says, this doesn't set out to do anything world-shattering, but the execution is very good indeed. The rituals find a very good balance of slightly unsettling, heavy with meaning and variety. I got more and more into Dr. Sinombre's character voice as we got to know her more, and her scrappy notes near the end were particularly effective and well-planned. Franco's history and personality felt sufficiently grounded to accept him, and I liked the tone of the early 80s interview (cutting it off so early felt like a bit of a cheat, but I could live with that). This set up an intriguing anomaly, and then used it to tell a good story. Nice work.







